{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as the rational basis for preferring better-corroborated theories, but it fails to address the well-known logical incoherence of Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude. As demonstrated by Tichý and Miller, under Popper's own criteria, a false theory with more content cannot be strictly compared to another false theory to determine which is 'closer' to the truth; the definition collapses when both theories have infinite true and false consequences. Since the problem explicitly asks if the preference is defensible on 'Popper's own terms,' invoking a concept that is logically untenable within his own formal system means the target has not successfully defended the preference without smuggling in extra-systemic assumptions or ignoring the internal contradiction.","premise":"Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude is logically incoherent because it fails to provide a valid ordering for false theories, making it impossible to rationally prefer one false theory over another as 'closer to truth' using only his defined terms.","premises_essential":["0a2387885e2c6c11f119cabac5ceb0f1d9ee188619283b9c6137189c39611265"],"successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be formally defined within Popper's system to rank false theories, what non-inductive metric does the target propose that remains consistent with Popper's rejection of probability?","target":"0a2387885e2c6c11f119cabac5ceb0f1d9ee188619283b9c6137189c39611265"}]}